  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "6.1.0 - Adds IDocumentClient.UpdateMetadataAsync(documentId, DocumentMetadataUpdateRequest): corrects the metadata of a document that is already stored - its properties, what they hold (metadataTypes) and its text - which previously could only be written by the upload that created the document, leaving SQL as the only way to fix one. Each part is optional and independent: null leaves it unchanged, a value replaces it whole, an empty dictionary clears it; declaring a type re-indexes the document so a meta[...] filter sees the new kind at once, and the answer carries the declarations back, which no other response does. Needs EditMetadata on the document or the metadata.write scope, and an ECM server from 19/08/2026. Everything below is unchanged from 6.0.0. - .NET client for the ECM (AppGateway) APIs: documents and their versions, files, tags and tag management, per-document ACL, lookups, tagging-rule sync and the outbox event feed, over the client-credentials + on-behalf token exchange, so every call is ACL-trimmed to the acting user. BREAKING, both caught at compile time: DocumentMetadataKeyFacetItemDto takes a fifth positional parameter, ValueKinds; and DocumentQuery.MetaFilters widened from IReadOnlyDictionary to a collection of key/value pairs so one property can carry two filters - assigning a Dictionary still compiles, reading the property back as one does not. New here: MetadataTypes on DocumentUploadRequest and DocumentBatchUploadRequest lets whoever writes a property declare what it holds (string, number, date, datetime, bool), which is how a reference number stops being indexed as a number; MetadataFilters with the Meta helpers (Meta.Is, Meta.Between, Meta.From(x).To(y); From/To include the bound, After/Before exclude it) builds metadata queries without hand-spelling operators, removing the three ways the raw string form fails silently outside an English locale - two ends of a range landing on different properties, a culture-formatted date, a comma decimal; and DocumentMetadataKeyFacetItemDto.ValueKinds says which kinds a property is indexed as before you build a range on it. Where documents land is decided by the credential: an ECM administrator grants an integration client one workspace, that grant travels in the token, and nothing on the client side names a workspace - an application writing to several declares one credential per workspace under Ecm:Connections, each resolved as a keyed IEcmClient. The retired configuration keys (Ecm:ClientCredentials:*, Ecm:DefaultWorkspace, Ecm:DefaultWorkspaceId, Ecm:WorkspaceMode, Ecm:ValidateWorkspaceOnStartup) are refused at startup with the replacement named rather than ignored silently, and startup logs which workspace each credential writes to. Tags split by audience: ecm.Tags for application code (no role, scoped by the server to the caller) and ecm.TagManagement for the organisation-wide taxonomy (admin or document.manager, or the tags.manage scope for software acting as itself - reads included, because that surface spans everyone's personal namespaces); includeAllNamespaces is a nullable bool defaulting to false, with Ecm:TagManagement:IncludeAllNamespaces for an application whose whole job is those screens. Ecm:Documents:IncludeTags and :IncludeMetadata set what a document list carries for the calls that do not say. services.AddHealthChecks().AddEcm() reports whether the integration is configured well enough to work, including a membership revoked after the token was minted; DocumentUploadRequest.IdempotencyKey makes a repeated upload safe. Multi-targets net8.0 and net10.0, with the shared contracts assembly bundled inside the package. Requires an ECM server from this release. See CHANGELOG.md, shipped in this package.",
